{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Over-Tides.}}⟦200⟧arise from the potential <math>V = \frac{m}{r^4}\rho^3(\tfrac{5}{2}\cos^3 z - \tfrac{3}{2}\cos z)</math>. They give rise to a small diurnal tide <math>\text{M}_1</math>, and to a small ter-diurnal tide <math>\text{M}_3</math>; but we shall not give the analytical development.

⟦300⟧§ 26. ''Over-Tides, Compound Tides and Meteorological Tides.''—We have in § 23 stated results derived from dynamical theory as
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Over-Tides.}}
to over-tides, which represent the change of profile of the wave as it advances in shallow water. The only tides in which it has hitherto been thought necessary to represent this change of form belong to the principal lunar and principal solar series. Thus, besides the fundamental astronomical tides <math>\text{M}_2</math> and <math>\text{S}_2</math>, the over-tides <math>\text{M}_4</math>, <math>\text{M}_6</math>, <math>\text{M}_8</math> and <math>\text{S}_4</math>, <math>\text{S}_6</math> are usually deduced by harmonic analysis.

⟦600⟧Compound tides have been also referred to in § 23; they represent a result of the combination of two waves of different speeds travel-
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Compound Tides.}}
ling through shallow water. On combining the speeds of the important tides, it will be found that there is in many cases a compound tide which has itself a speed identical with that of an astronomical or meteorological tide. We thus find that the tides <math>\text{O}</math>, <math>\text{K}_1</math>, <math>\text{P}</math>, <math>\text{M}_2</math>, <math>\text{Mf}</math>, <math>\text{Q}</math>, <math>\text{M}_1</math>, <math>\text{L}</math> are liable to perturbation in shallow water. We refer to the ''Brit. Assoc. Report'' for 1883 or to Harris's ''Manual'' for a schedule, with initials, of the compound tides which are usually evaluated.

⟦900⟧All tides whose period is an exact multiple or submultiple of a mean solar day, or of a tropical year, are affected by meteorological
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Meteorological Tides.}}
conditions. Thus all the tides of the principal solar astronomical series <math>\text{S}</math>, with speeds <math>\gamma-\eta</math>, <math>2(\gamma-\eta)</math>, <math>3(\gamma-\eta)</math>, &c., are subject to more or less meteorological perturbation. An annual inequality in the diurnal meteorological tide <math>\text{S}_1</math> will also give rise to a tide <math>\gamma-2\eta</math>, and this will be fused with and indistinguishable from the astronomical <math>\text{P}</math>; it will also give rise to a tide with speed <math>\gamma</math>, which will be indistinguishable from the astronomical part of <math>\text{K}_1</math>. Similarly the astronomical tide <math>\text{K}_2</math> may be perturbed by a semi-annual inequality in the semi-diurnal astronomical tide of speed <math>2(\gamma-\eta)</math>. Although the diurnal tide <math>\text{S}_1</math> or <math>\gamma-\eta</math> and the semi-annual and annual tides of speeds <math>2\eta</math> and <math>\eta</math> are all quite insensible as arising from astronomical causes, yet they have been found of sufficient importance to be considered. The annual and semi-annual tides are of enormous importance in some rivers, representing in fact the yearly flooding in the rainy season. In the reduction of these tides the arguments of the <math>\text{S}</math> series are <math>t</math>, <math>2t</math>, <math>3t</math>, &c., and of the annual, semi-annual, ter-annual tides <math>h</math>, <math>2h</math>, <math>3h</math>. As far as can be foreseen, the magnitudes of these tides are constant from year to year.

⟦1600⟧§ 27. ''On the Form of Presentation of Results of Tidal Observations.''—Supposing <math>n</math> to be the speed of any tide in degrees per mean solar
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Immediate Result of Harmonic Analysis.}}
hour, and <math>t</math> to be mean solar time elapsing since <math>0^h</math> of the first day of (say) a year of continuous observation, then the immediate result of harmonic analysis is to obtain a height <math>\text{R}</math> and an angle <math>\zeta</math> such that the height of this tide at the time <math>t</math> is given by
{{block center|<math>\text{R}\cos(nt-\zeta)</math>.}}
<math>\text{R}</math> is the semi-range of the tide (say) in British feet, and <math>\zeta</math> is an angle such that <math>\zeta/n</math> is the time elapsing after <math>0^h</math> of the first day until it is high water of this particular tide. It is obvious that <math>\zeta</math> may have any value from <math>0^\circ</math> to <math>360^\circ</math>, and that the results of the analysis of successive years of observation will not be comparable with one another when presented in this form.

⟦2100⟧But let us suppose that the results of the analysis are presented in a number of terms of the form
{{block center|<math>\text{fH}\cos(V+u-\kappa)</math>,}}
where <math>V</math> is a linear function of the moon's and sun's mean longitudes, the mean longitude of the moon's and sun's perigees, and
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Final Form; Tidal Constants.}}
the local mean solar time at the place of observation, reduced to angle at <math>15^\circ</math> per hour. <math>V</math> increases uniformly with the time, and its rate of increase per mean solar hour is the <math>n</math> of the first method, and is called the speed of the tide. It is supposed that <math>u</math> stands for a certain function of the longitude of the node of the lunar orbit at an epoch half a year later than <math>0^h</math> of the first day. Strictly speaking, <math>u</math> should be taken as the same function of the longitude of the moon's node, varying as the node moves; but, as the variation is but small in the course of a year, <math>u</math> may be treated as a constant and put equal to an average value for the year, which average value is taken as the true value of <math>u</math> at exactly mid year. Together <math>V+u</math> constitute that function which has been tabulated as the "argument" in the schedules of § 25. Since <math>V+u</math> are together the whole argument according to the equilibrium theory of tides, with sea covering the whole earth, it follows that <math>\kappa/n</math> is the lagging of the tide which arises from kinetic action, friction of the water, imperfect elasticity of the earth, and the distribution of land It is supposed that <math>\text{H}</math> is the mean value in British feet of the semi-range of the particular tide in question; <math>\text{f}</math> is a numerical factor of augmentation or diminution, due to the variability of the obliquity of the lunar orbit. The value of <math>\text{f}</math> is the ratio of the "coefficient" in the third column of the preceding schedules to the mean value of the same term. It is obvious, then, that, if the tidal observations are consistent from year to year, <math>\text{H}</math> and <math>\kappa</math> should

